{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8919637576a664da","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'human_process_gates is scored inconsistently across structurally identical\n10\t    gated implementations: E004 (-1) and E007 (-1) cite stakeholder confirmation and\n11\t    user validation that are the gating Investigation''s ACs or acceptance activities,\n12\t    while E005/E006 (0) correctly classify the same structure as not a process gate.\n13\t    Align the scoring — E004/E007 should be 0 or E005/E006 should be -1.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n16\t  description: E007 component/team assignment (Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard) is a disclosed\n17\t    placeholder pending E003; confirm reassignment against E003's outcome (already\n18\t    captured in E007 gate_failure_impact).\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Completeness\n21\t  description: If E002's boundary decision includes the strategy's fourth candidate\n22\t    (first deployed model), no epic in the set can absorb serving-side onboarding\n23\t    friction — E005/E006 fallbacks only rewrite scope within their own components.\n24\t    Verify the confirmed boundary against the two-layer epic structure.\n25\terror: null\n26\t---\n27\t## Review Summary\n28\t\n29\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n30\tRecommendation: accept\n31\t\n32\tThis decomposition handles a deliberately underspecified strategy (three HLRs\n33\twithout priorities, three open questions, no ACs, no teams table, no\n34\tout-of-scope boundary) correctly: each genuine unknown becomes an Investigation\n35\tepic that gates its downstream implementation with a populated\n36\t`gate_failure_impact`, the three investigations run in parallel, implementations\n37\tare split along component/team boundaries, and the docs-authoring epic runs last.\n38\tThe health warnings section surfaces every defect in the strategy rather than\n39\tsilently papering over it. One criterion loses a point for inconsistent\n40\thuman-process-gate scoring across structurally identical epics.\n41\t\n42\t## Criterion Details\n43\t\n44\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n45\t\n46\tAll four HLRs are covered and traceable: HLR-1 → E001+E004, HLR-2 →\n47\tE002+E005+E006, HLR-3 → E003+E007, HLR-4 → E008. The traceability matrix is\n48\tpresent in the decomposition summary and each epic carries an HLR Traceability\n49\tsection. HLR-1/2/3 carry no priority in the strategy; defaulting them to P1 with\n50\tan explicit rationale (leadership urgency) and a health warning to confirm is a\n51\treasonable, disclosed default rather than a priority inheritance error. Priority\n52\tinheritance is sound: each P1 Investigation gates P1 implementations at the same\n53\tlevel, and E008 (docs-authoring) correctly derives P2 from the strategy's\n54\t`priority: Normal` via the docs-authoring exception rather than inheriting from\n55\tthe P0/P1 implementations it depends on. No priority collapse: each epic maps to\n56\ta single HLR, and the P2 whitepaper is in its own deferrable epic.\n57\t\n58\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n59\t\n60\tThe DAG is acyclic and every edge is justified. Frontmatter `dependencies` and\n61\tthe summary diagram match exactly in both directions (E004←E001, E005/E006←E002,\n62\tE007←E003, E008←E004/E005/E006/E007), with arrows drawn dependency→dependent per\n63\tconvention. The three Investigation gates are Rule 3 true gates — each resolves a\n64\tstrategy open question that determines downstream scope or existence. The four\n65\tRule 11 edges into the docs-authoring epic are valid and correctly do not trigger\n66\tpriority inheritance. Implementations are not serialized against each other\n67\t(different components/areas, no shared build artifacts), so the parallel-eligible\n68\twork is genuinely parallel. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or\n69\tlicense-validation epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings do not apply. Critical\n70\tpath length 3 (Investigation → Implementation → docs-authoring) is appropriate\n71\tfor a strategy of this size. No data/artifact dependency is missing from the DAG:\n72\tevery implementation consumes only its gating investigation's decision record\n73\tand E008 consumes all four implementations — all captured as edges. No branch\n74\tfiles are used, and the justification for not emitting them (the HLR-1 conflict's\n75\tresolution space is not bounded to ≤3 enumerable epic structures, since a tiered\n76\treconciliation could satisfy both accounts) is sound; the `gated_by` +\n77\t`gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` modeling is the correct alternative.\n78\t\n79\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n80\t\n81\tComponent/team tuples are clean: E001-E005 and E007 are Dashboard/RHOAI\n82\tDashboard, E006 is Notebooks/Workbenches, E008 is Documentation/RHOAI\n83\tDocumentation. No epic bundles multiple components or teams. The\n84\tconsole/workbench split of HLR-2 (E005 vs E006) is drawn along the owning-team\n85\tboundary with the gating investigation assigning friction items to each side —\n86\tnot a duplication. No scope duplication between siblings: E004 (admin config),\n87\tE005 (console onboarding), E006 (workbench onboarding), and E007 (tooling\n88\tintegration) own disjoint surfaces. Work-product types are not mixed: every\n89\timplementation epic explicitly excludes new doc-topic authoring (incidental\n90\ttouch-ups only), and all content authoring lives in E008. Epic sizes are bounded\n91\tby their gating investigations' decision records rather than pre-committed, which\n92\tis the right shape for this strategy.\n93\t\n94\tMinor (disclosed, acceptable): E007's Dashboard assignment is a placeholder — the\n95\taffected component is unknowable until E003 resolves. The decomposition states\n96\tthis explicitly and builds reassignment into E007's `gate_failure_impact`, which\n97\tis the correct handling; the assignment should still be confirmed against E003's\n98\toutcome.\n99\t\n100\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n101\t\n102\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's direction\n103\tdecision determines what E004 builds, E002's boundary decision determines E005's\n104\tand E006's scope (and existence), E003's tool identification determines E007's\n105\texistence, scope, and even owning team. Each Investigation has downstream\n106\tdependents. All four Implementations produce concrete artifacts. Every epic with\n107\ta non-null `gated_by` (E004, E005, E006, E007) has a `gate_failure_impact` with\n108\tboth `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target\n109\tappears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list. No epic lists an Investigation\n110\tin `dependencies` without `gated_by`. No misclassifications.\n111\t\n112\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n113\t\n114\tSignal fields are type-correct (Investigation epics carry\n115\t`investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`), all eight\n116\tai-signals files exist, and frontmatter values match the rationale tables. Most\n117\tvalues are well justified — notably the honest `source_accessibility: 0` /\n118\t`human_judgment_required: -2` on E001 and E003 (the answers live in field-team\n119\tchannels and stakeholder calls, not readable artifacts) and `change_specificity:\n120\t-1` / `open_questions: -1` on all gated implementations.\n121\t\n122\tTwo signal values are arguable because they are inconsistent with the treatment\n123\tof structurally identical epics: E004 and E007 score\n124\t`human_process_gates: -1` partly on grounds that apply equally to E005/E006,\n125\twhich score 0. E004's rationale cites the direction-decision stakeholder\n126\tconfirmation — but that confirmation is E001's acceptance criterion and has\n127\talready passed by the time E004 runs; E005 is gated by the identical\n128\tstructure (E002's \"scope decision is confirmed with the strategy owner\") yet\n129\tscores 0. E004 and E007 also cite representative-user validation as a human\n130\tapproval step, while E005's rationale explicitly classifies the same kind of\n131\tvalidation as \"an acceptance activity, not a process gate\" (score 0). E007\n132\tadditionally cites the recommendation's strategy-owner confirmation, which is\n133\tE003's AC, not E007's. Either E004/E007 should be 0 or E005/E006 should be -1 —\n134\tthe same gating-plus-user-validation structure must score the same way. This\n135\tdoes not change any epic's band enough to alter `ai_implementability` outcomes\n136\tmaterially, so it is minor.\n137\t\n138\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n139\t\n140\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present where\n141\tapplicable: E004 (which may replace existing admin configuration behavior) has\n142\tan explicit rollback/feature-flag AC; E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical\n143\treview AC plus publication through the docs process; no konflux-chain epics\n144\texist so the build-pipeline AC does not apply. The strategy provides no ACs of\n145\tits own, and the per-epic ACs are correctly anchored to the gating\n146\tinvestigations' decision records (e.g., \"matches the direction and surfaces\n147\tenumerated in the investigation's confirmed decision\") rather than inventing\n148\tunsupported specifics — this is disclosed in the health warnings. No epic body\n149\treferences sibling epics by bare draft ID; all references use full\n150\tRHAISTRAT-9008-Ennn IDs.\n151\t\n152\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n153\t\n154\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: the feedback-consolidation problem-statement\n155\tcontext is a deliverable of all three investigations, each of the three open\n156\tquestions maps to exactly one Investigation epic (documented in the\n157\tInvestigation Treatment table), HLR-4's whitepaper is E008, and the missing\n158\tout-of-scope boundary is compensated by E003's explicit not-pursuing list.\n159\tStrategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped — the\n160\tdecomposition's health warnings enumerate all seven strategy defects (contradictory\n161\tHLR-1, unmeasurable HLR-2 target, unprioritized HLRs, vague HLR-3, missing\n162\tsections, no out-of-scope boundary, unconsolidated feedback) and each is handled\n163\tby a concrete mechanism in the epic set. E008 covers the documentation\n164\trequirement even though the strategy lacks a Prerequisites & Process Gates\n165\tsection. Cross-epic consistency holds: E002's four boundary candidates flow into\n166\tE005 (console) and E006 (workbench).\n167\t\n168\tMinor: if E002's boundary decision includes \"first deployed model\" (one of the\n169\tfour candidates the strategy enumerates), no epic in the set can absorb\n170\tserving-side onboarding friction — E005's and E006's fallback approaches only\n171\trewrite scope within their own components, and E002's friction categorization\n172\tpresumes a two-way console/workbench split. This is acceptable given the\n173\tstrategy's Affected Components list only Dashboard and Notebooks, but the\n174\trevision/confirmation step should verify the boundary decision against the epic\n175\tset's two-layer structure.\n176\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87fb09261067101a","agent_id":"a2ffe5240e4caaf98","session_id":"cc08ccf1-892e-454d-8ed1-68ebd3e76220","uuid":"dfa292dc-3c05-4386-838b-137d01b58c5a","timestamp":"2026-10-08T16:49:34.623Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8919637576a664da","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
