in scope. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n75\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 3 | No enterprise tools are named in the strategy; the integration epic's existence and scope depend on the identified tool list. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n76\t| E004 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Whitepaper describes delivered usability improvements; blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n77\t| E005 → E008 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n78\t| E006 → E008 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n79\t| E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n80\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 / independent unknowns | The three open questions are independent of each other; investigations run in parallel. |\n81\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ E005 ∥ E006 ∥ E007 (post-gate) | Rules 14/15 | Implementations in different components/repos or different areas; no shared build-against artifacts. |\n82\t\n83\tdocs-authoring priority exception applied to E008: strategy frontmatter\n84\t`priority: Normal` → P2. It does not push priority upstream.\n85\t\n86\t## Investigation Treatment of Open Questions\n87\t\n88\t| Open question (from strategy) | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n89\t|---|---|---|\n90\t| 1. Which accounts' feedback takes precedence for HLR-1 (conflicting directions)? | Yes — determines the scope of the admin-configuration implementation | Investigation epic E001, gating E004 |\n91\t| 2. What does \"onboarding\" cover for HLR-2? | Yes — determines which onboarding implementations exist and what they target | Investigation epic E002, gating E005 and E006 |\n92\t| 3. Which enterprise tools does HLR-3 mean? | Yes — determines whether integration epics exist, for which tools, and on which components | Investigation epic E003, gating E007 |\n93\t\n94\t**No conditional branch files were emitted.** Although HLR-1's conflict has two\n95\tstated poles, the resolution space is not bounded to ≤3 enumerable\n96\tepic-structure outcomes (a reconciliation — e.g. tiered defaults plus advanced\n97\tper-project controls — would satisfy both accounts and change scope rather than\n98\tselect between two pre-drawn structures). The gating is therefore modeled with\n99\t`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on each downstream\n100\timplementation rather than branch epics.\n101\t\n102\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n103\t\n104\t| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Epics | Coverage |\n105\t|-----|--------------------|-------|----------|\n106\t| HLR-1 — Administration should be simpler (conflicting directions) | — (unspecified) | E001, E004 | Investigation + gated implementation |\n107\t| HLR-2 — New data-scientist onboarding under a day (scope undefined) | — (unspecified) | E002, E005, E006 | Investigation + gated implementations (console + workbench layers) |\n108\t| HLR-3 — Better integration with enterprise tooling (tools unnamed) | — (unspecified) | E003, E007 | Investigation + gated implementation |\n109\t| HLR-4 — Publish enterprise usability best practices whitepaper | P2 | E008 | docs-authoring epic |\n110\t\n111\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n112\t\n113\t**Priority defaults:** HLR-1/2/3 carry no priority in the strategy. They were\n114\tdefaulted to **P1** based on the problem statement's \"Leadership wants visible\n115\tusability progress this release\". This default must be confirmed by the strategy\n116\towner (see Health Warnings).\n117\t\n118\t## Component Mapping Notes\n119\t\n120\t- Strategy's Affected Components are a bare list (no change descriptions, no\n121\t  owners). Both map directly to canonical components: **Dashboard** and\n122\t  **Notebooks**.\n123\t- Owner teams derived from architecture context: Dashboard → **RHOAI Dashboard**;\n124\t  Notebooks → **Workbenches**.\n125\t- E007 (enterprise tooling integration) is assigned to **Dashboard** as a\n126\t  placeholder: the affected component cannot be known until the tool\n127\t  identification investigation resolves. Reassign if the identified tools point\n128\t  elsewhere (captured in its gate failure impact).\n129\t- E008 (whitepaper) maps to the canonical **Documentation** component; the\n130\t  owning team is not stated anywhere in the strategy (Impacted Teams table\n131\t  missing) and was set to RHOAI Documentation — confirm with strategy owner.\n132\t- E002 is assigned to the RHOAI Dashboard team because onboarding is a\n133\t  cross-component journey whose console surface the Dashboard team owns; the\n134\t  workbench-side follow-through lives in E006 (Workbenches team).\n135\t\n136\t## Health Warnings\n137\t\n138\t1. **Contradictory requirement (HLR-1):** one account wants fewer exposed\n139\t   configuration options, another wants more fine-grained per-project controls.\n140\t   Not resolvable by decomposition; gated on Investigation E001.\n141\t2. **Unmeasurable acceptance target (HLR-2):** \"less than a day\" cannot be\n142\t   tested until onboarding scope is defined and a baseline measured (E002).\n143\t   Scope trap if implementation epics were created without the gate.\n144\t3. **Unprioritized HLRs:** HLR-1/2/3 have no priority. Defaulted to P1 from the\n145\t   leadership-urgency statement; confirm.\n146\t4. **Vague requirement (HLR-3):** no enterprise tools named; \"integrate better\"\n147\t   is undefined. E007's component assignment is a placeholder pending E003.\n148\t5. **Missing strategy sections:** Impacted Teams, Dependencies table,\n149\t   Acceptance Criteria, NFRs — per-epic ACs are derived from HLRs plus standard\n150\t   implementation requirements; they are necessarily thinner than\n151\t   strategy-provided Given/When/Then ACs would be.\n152\t6. **No explicit out-of-scope boundary:** \"Nothing has been explicitly scoped\n153\t   out.\" Boundary risk for the integration epic in particular (enterprise\n154\t   tooling could expand indefinitely); E003's deliverable includes a bounded,\n155\t   prioritized recommendation.\n156\t7. **Unconsolidated feedback source:** the problem statement notes feedback\n157\t   arrived through different channels and is pa